WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:08.500
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Right, may we then move on to the next item on the agenda, which is the

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.040
17-18 / 18-19 operational planning and contracting round.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.120
Matthew will will you be introducing this?

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:24.920
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Just to remind you that
we brought forward the contracting round by

00:00:24.929 --> 00:00:33.360
three months this year, so that our ambition
is to close it and complete it by the end

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:40.470
of December; and that this year, we are letting
two-year contracts rather than one-year contracts

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:46.830
in order to give the NHS more certainty, and
to be able to put in place a longer-term view

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:53.190
of the way that services need to develop.
Those discussions between commissioners and

00:00:53.190 --> 00:01:00.210
providers are going on across the country
as we speak. We are beginning to see some

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:06.850
contracts landing around the country, and
some of our major specialist commissioning

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:12.450
agreements, we are getting some feedback that
all the details have been agreed; it is just

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:16.650
a question of getting the paperwork sorted
out, so there is a lot of positive work going

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:22.000
on. Clearly in the financial situation and
with the pressures on the NHS, these are not

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.759
easy conversations. We are trying to get the
balance between investment in prevention and

00:01:26.759 --> 00:01:32.210
out-of-hospital services right, with the investment
in hospital services, in order to provide

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:40.600
the best care possible. I think that, while
those are difficult conversations and professional

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:47.689
people are doing tough jobs, our sense is
that, in the vast majority of places, the

00:01:47.689 --> 00:01:54.139
work that started in the STPs around thinking
about the needs of a whole population and

00:01:54.139 --> 00:01:57.909
where we are going to be in four to five years'
time have carried through into the contracting

00:01:57.909 --> 00:02:05.859
round, so it is a more collaborative contracting
round this year than sometimes. Our regional

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:11.490
offices and local teams, both from our teams
and from NHS Improvement, are working closely

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:19.500
all over the country trying to steer these
agreements into the best possible place, and

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.610
hopefully we will get as many of those closed,
and then we have an escalation process through

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:30.170
our joint regional offices with NHSI, through
to national directors. For the very special

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:35.450
ones who need extra help, Simon and Jim Mackey
will personally be helping them bring their

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:40.110
contracts to an agreement, which will be a
positive experience for everybody.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:46.780
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much for that
characterisation.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:56.320
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Maybe I could just add two
things, Malcolm, one of which is just to underline

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:00.730
the importance of the earlier timeline in
another respect, which is, as I will say when

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:05.510
I come to the financial report for this year,
the big issue we have is being ambitious about

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:13.350
efficiency improvement and having enough critical,
well-worked-through plans to deliver on that.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:17.030
With a cycle which finishes the planning in
March, there is not a hell of a lot of time

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:21.870
to get mobilised for a year that starts in
April. The intention this year is very much

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:26.760
to do the planning so that it concludes around
about now, and we have then got three of what

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.041
I am describing as quite 'golden' months;
I am sure they will not necessarily feel like

00:03:31.041 --> 00:03:35.030
that when we are in them, but they are golden
months that we have not had in previous years,

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:39.630
to get really focused on how I mobilise all
of the things that I have planned for in a

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:43.850
way that they start delivering in April rather
than delivering progressively through the

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:45.120
year. That is the first thing.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:51.810
The second thing is a new challenge for us,
I think, as we go into next year, which is

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:57.070
all about the way in which we manage risk
across the system, so we have very much constructed

00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:02.410
the plans both to share the risk mitigation
across whole health economies; it is the way

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:06.940
the STPs thought about their business. If
you think just about the way in which we have

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:11.610
constructed the risk reserve for next year,
it is not just the commissioning sector that

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:16.600
is putting aside money for that; there is
a joint ownership of it through the part of

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:22.210
it that the commissioners put in, 0.5% next
year; there is part of the CQUIN earnings

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:27.430
of providers that are being put into the reserves;
and then we have our own contribution from

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:31.919
drawdown. This is carefully crafted to make
sure of two things, really, one of which is

00:04:31.919 --> 00:04:36.319
to ensure that we can get as much money as
possible actually being spent next year on

00:04:36.319 --> 00:04:41.770
the priorities that we need to do; but secondly,
that there is joint ownership of the risks

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:47.699
and the means of dealing with the risks. That
then allies itself, we expect, to a much more

00:04:47.699 --> 00:04:51.879
joined-up way in which we manage those risks
across health economies next year. I think

00:04:51.879 --> 00:04:59.479
those are just two facets that are worth drawing
out of the differences in the process this

00:04:59.479 --> 00:05:00.479
year.

00:05:00.479 --> 00:05:03.870
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you. This was an update
paper, just to keep us up to speed with where

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:19.289
things have been progressing. Again, thank
you, Matthew, for the paper, and for your

00:05:19.289 --> 00:05:22.389
remarks on it.

00:05:22.389 --> 00:05:33.430
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: All 44 STPs submitted
their first pass of plans and proposals towards

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:42.379
the end of October, and they cover a wide
range of comprehensive sets of plans and thoughts

00:05:42.379 --> 00:05:47.889
aimed at delivering the Five Year Forward
View, aimed at improving health, improving

00:05:47.889 --> 00:05:54.180
quality and improving the productivity of
the NHS, and with a particular focus everywhere

00:05:54.180 --> 00:06:03.740
on prevention and avoiding unnecessary hospital
admissions, so care closer to home, and trying

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:11.389
to rebalance services towards optimising for
people in terms of where they receive their

00:06:11.389 --> 00:06:18.050
care and where they receive support. We have
now had nearly all of the STPs published;

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:24.849
last time I checked, 40 out of the 44 were
out. We are expecting all 44 to be out by

00:06:24.849 --> 00:06:31.949
Christmas, so these are now in the public
domain and there is discussion going on across

00:06:31.949 --> 00:06:40.300
the country; you see it happening in local
newspapers. I think whilst there is a degree

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:46.949
of noise, there is also quite a lot of engagement
around it as well. The development of the

00:06:46.949 --> 00:06:54.689
proposals as they currently sit I think have
been a very significant joint effort by the

00:06:54.689 --> 00:06:59.169
various parts of the NHS and our colleagues
in local government, and we are grateful for

00:06:59.169 --> 00:07:04.219
the support of the LGA right through this
process in helping us build that engagement.

00:07:04.219 --> 00:07:11.729
However, as we move from the proposal phase
that we are in at the moment into turning

00:07:11.729 --> 00:07:20.550
these into plans to be implemented, I think
it is important that the local STPs need to

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:28.669
widen the tent of the conversation; we need
to increase the level of clinical engagement,

00:07:28.669 --> 00:07:32.680
we need to increase the involvement of the
public and the voluntary sector, we need to

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:38.400
increase the involvement of elected local
councillors and of our private-sector partners

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:41.150
who are helping deliver the NHS locally.

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:45.740
Every STP you go to has done degrees of that
in different places; some have had fantastic

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:50.860
engagement with local councillors, others
less so. However, we need to create a standardised

00:07:50.860 --> 00:08:00.949
feeling now that this is part of a joint engagement
in how we take the NHS forward over the next

00:08:00.949 --> 00:08:08.819
four years, and where the key priorities for
local place-based decisions about the priorities

00:08:08.819 --> 00:08:12.139
for local health systems happen.

00:08:12.139 --> 00:08:20.379
As we run out of the end of the contracting
round and into January, we would expect firstly

00:08:20.379 --> 00:08:25.520
that engagement, that widening of the engagement,
to be starting to happen. We would expect

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:37.519
to see some of the proposals turning into
plans. All of the STPs have two parts to them:

00:08:37.519 --> 00:08:41.979
there is stuff we have to do immediately,
and stuff we need to put in place now in order

00:08:41.979 --> 00:08:46.350
that, in two or three years' time, you are
seeing the change in the services. So, we

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:50.610
need to see those two tracks coming forward:
the things that need to happen in order to

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:56.149
improve health and improve efficiency going
into next year starting, but also the work

00:08:56.149 --> 00:09:01.810
which means that we are ready for the two
years that happen after that. There will be

00:09:01.810 --> 00:09:04.779
some consultations that need to happen in
some parts of the country where major change

00:09:04.779 --> 00:09:14.480
is going on, and we need to create the way
of joint working that carries us forward beyond

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:21.579
the writing of the document. STPs are not
statutory organisations, and we are asking

00:09:21.579 --> 00:09:27.240
them to come together and effectively choose
to pool some of their decision-making rights

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:32.922
in order to make difficult decisions and complex
decisions, when you look across the local

00:09:32.922 --> 00:09:37.269
health service and you say, �No individual
system can solve this on their own; we need

00:09:37.269 --> 00:09:41.940
the hospital and primary care, and social
care, and the community services to work together

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:47.889
in order to achieve this thing.' How do the
STPs create the decision-making mechanisms

00:09:47.889 --> 00:09:51.920
which allow them to jointly align a decision
which then means you can go back into your

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:55.790
piece and make that happen, but you are doing
it as part of a coherent whole?

00:09:55.790 --> 00:10:02.540
I know that is something that is being much
debated across the country at the moment,

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:09.519
and our regional offices are trying to support
the evolution of those structures which say:

00:10:09.519 --> 00:10:13.380
accountability still lies where it lies because
that is what the law says, but to do what

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:17.720
the people need, you need to be able to pool
decision-making and how you share that, and

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:23.540
I think making that work well is going to
be the critical part of moving from a plan

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:27.410
to moving to execution.

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:32.540
As we move into that stage, we are going to
need to reset the plans with where the contracting

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:37.709
round ends in order that you are taking off
from the place you thought you were taking

00:10:37.709 --> 00:10:43.410
off from. We have tried to make sure that
the STPs are involved in the contracting round

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:49.389
as we have been going along, but there is
that step-change, and then getting up to speed

00:10:49.389 --> 00:10:53.400
and moving quickly, because there is an awful
lot to do in the golden three months that

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:58.610
we are all looking forward to, Paul, but there
is an awful lot to do next year if we are

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:00.080
going to make the decisions.

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:04.370
We have some decisions that we need to do
as well, so we need to make some decisions

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:09.720
around capital, we need to make some decisions
about IT investment, and we are going through

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:16.839
an exercise now internally to say: the resources
that are in CCGs, the resources that are in

00:11:16.839 --> 00:11:21.670
the Commissioning Support Units and the resources
that NHS England has out in the field, how

00:11:21.670 --> 00:11:27.399
do we best align those to support the STPs?
If that is going to be a key part of how we

00:11:27.399 --> 00:11:32.839
deliver change, then we need to be able to
get the leaders in that area to be using the

00:11:32.839 --> 00:11:38.570
people that we have access to as part of the
force that is driving this forward. That piece

00:11:38.570 --> 00:11:44.680
of work is going on now. Also, about how we
get more bodies aligned around a common direction;

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:49.430
and also, to be picking up the national strategies
like the cancer strategies and making sure

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:58.420
that they are being delivered and continue
to be delivered on the ground.

00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:09.620
&gt;&gt;Michelle Mitchell: There has been some comment
on the perceived lack of engagement with citizens,

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:16.250
voluntary sector and local communities in
the STP programme. Could you talk us through

00:12:16.250 --> 00:12:25.290
specifically how NHS England will support
the STP programme to improve the engagement

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:32.949
locally, and I think also set expectations
around engagement with local communities and

00:12:32.949 --> 00:12:33.949
citizens?

00:12:33.949 --> 00:12:40.660
The second part is, can you give assurance
how NHS England will meet its legal requirements

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:45.509
through consultation and engagement with citizens
through this process? Thank you.

00:12:45.509 --> 00:12:51.170
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes, I think there are
a couple of elements to that. One is to say

00:12:51.170 --> 00:13:00.220
that for every area where engagement with
the voluntary sector has not been strong or

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:05.569
engagement with local councils has not been
strong, there is another one where it has

00:13:05.569 --> 00:13:10.620
been fantastic, and that in part reflects
where they have come from and the nature of

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:16.880
the issues that they are dealing with, and
what has happened in the past. I think to

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:21.900
have got to where we have got to has been
a pretty good show by the STPs; I would be

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:26.980
confident in saying that across the 44 STPs,
there are none of them who have not got better

00:13:26.980 --> 00:13:32.100
working relationships now than they had when
they started, but there is still a journey

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:41.910
to go on. Where we have to move to now is
a general sense of engagement and communication,

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:47.060
and a plan that builds on all of the strengths
in an area, so being able to hear what the

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:54.329
public feel their priorities are, but also
being able to engage properly with the voluntary

00:13:54.329 --> 00:13:59.480
sector, the charity sector, who are delivering
an awful lot of healthcare in this country,

00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:03.350
and have a lot to offer going forward.

00:14:03.350 --> 00:14:10.209
Through our regional teams and through the
guidance of the STPs, we will be encouraging

00:14:10.209 --> 00:14:15.569
and facilitating that better dialogue and
the enhancement of that dialogue, so that

00:14:15.569 --> 00:14:21.779
there is a joint sense of a way forward. That
does not mean that every area will be unanimous

00:14:21.779 --> 00:14:26.139
on what needs to be done, because there are
some tricky decisions that need to be taken,

00:14:26.139 --> 00:14:29.640
but we need to hear all of the voices as we
steer through that.

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:36.500
Then, as you say, there are specific changes
for which we need to properly consult, as

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:43.220
in there is a statutory framework. We are
very clear that there is nothing in the STPs

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:48.290
that takes away the obligation to consult
over a major service change. What it ought

00:14:48.290 --> 00:14:53.630
to do is ensure that, when we do that consultation,
it is set in a broader strategic framework,

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:58.279
so you understand, 'Why this thing', because
it is part of a bigger story, rather than

00:14:58.279 --> 00:15:04.720
it just being the element on its own. We will
be doing that, and Jane, you might want to

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:09.571
add something to that. Jane's team are gearing
up to being able to provide advice and guidance

00:15:09.571 --> 00:15:15.949
on being innovative about the way in which
you engage with your population, and it not

00:15:15.949 --> 00:15:18.199
just being the way we have always done it.

00:15:18.199 --> 00:15:24.480
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Building on what Matthew
said, the patient and public engagement team

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:32.380
have actually held quite a few events across
the country with STPs, talking to them about

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:37.389
engagement and ways of doing it. They have
developed some support tools and some guidance

00:15:37.389 --> 00:15:44.870
and help to do that. Alongside that, we have
been talking to the team centrally about how

00:15:44.870 --> 00:15:50.839
they can provide bespoke and expert advice
to some of those STPs, and do that on different

00:15:50.839 --> 00:15:55.790
levels depending on what the local needs are.
So, there is a very clear expectation about

00:15:55.790 --> 00:16:01.790
what our 13Q statutory requirements are around
consultation and engagement, but there are

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:07.439
also the plans in place to actually provide
additional bespoke support, both through the

00:16:07.439 --> 00:16:12.810
regions but also from the central team where
some of the expertise lies, and we have been

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:15.129
working on that over the last few months.

00:16:15.129 --> 00:16:23.319
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Might I just add to Michelle's
and Jane's helpful questions and contributions

00:16:23.319 --> 00:16:26.529
that the Task Force that you have asked me
to chair is hopefully going to be bringing

00:16:26.529 --> 00:16:32.600
a kind of state-of-the-nation report to the
Board, to allow us to reflect on lessons learned

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:40.491
and where to go forward. This is a critical
area for NHS England to get right, and we

00:16:40.491 --> 00:16:49.850
are learning as we go on, so why do we not
bring the learning to date?

00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:53.540
The question I was going to ask is about our
relationship with local government which,

00:16:53.540 --> 00:17:02.019
as far as I am aware, this is the first real
attempt - and there have been a number - for

00:17:02.019 --> 00:17:06.470
NHS England to work hand in hand in local
government, so I am not surprised that we

00:17:06.470 --> 00:17:11.529
have patchy relationships in different places.
What we are keen to know, though, is what

00:17:11.529 --> 00:17:16.540
have we learned about our relationship with
local government and how we work with local

00:17:16.540 --> 00:17:24.079
government that we can bank and take forward
into future strategic attempts to bring health,

00:17:24.079 --> 00:17:29.120
social care and the political framework together?
I guess this is as much a question for Simon

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:33.690
as it is for you, but it seems to me that
if we cannot learn from where we are at, then

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:37.100
we are doomed to make any mistakes that we
have made again.

00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:41.281
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Even more elements next
time, yes. I think it is a really good question,

00:17:41.281 --> 00:17:51.809
and I think we are on a learning journey.
I suspect a lot of NHS managers have travelled

00:17:51.809 --> 00:17:57.700
down the same road that I have, so I understand
a lot better the inter-relationship between

00:17:57.700 --> 00:18:06.240
elected members and council officials now
than I did when I started this process, and

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:11.419
how closely they work together, and that there
is not really a dialogue with one without

00:18:11.419 --> 00:18:17.470
a dialogue with the other. I think it is easy
from an NHS point of view to view the elected

00:18:17.470 --> 00:18:23.230
council as being the board, if you like, and
it really does not work like that. So, we

00:18:23.230 --> 00:18:26.710
are learning on that journey, and some of
our areas have been doing it for a long time

00:18:26.710 --> 00:18:32.659
and are a lot smarter about it than I am,
but I just know from my experience over the

00:18:32.659 --> 00:18:37.970
last six months of being much more closely
involved in these kinds of discussions that

00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:43.000
our governance structures and local government
governance structures are different, and we

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:46.140
need to find a way that they can live together
sensibly.

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:51.679
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: If it helps, I have it in
mind to bring one or two senior local government

00:18:51.679 --> 00:18:55.560
leaders to a future meeting of the Board,
and we can take some of these issues together.

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:56.630
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Matthew, I would like to pick
up the point you made earlier about governance.

00:18:56.630 --> 00:19:08.500
As you know, we meet with all the chairs of
the clinical commissioning groups on a periodic

00:19:08.500 --> 00:19:12.470
basis, and you will not be surprised to know
there is quite a lot of anxiety out there

00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:19.350
as they move into uncharted territory on governance
and are faced with a requirement to maintain

00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:23.960
their statutory accountabilities while working
more collaboratively in partnership with other

00:19:23.960 --> 00:19:28.770
organisations that maybe think about things
quite differently from the way they do. I

00:19:28.770 --> 00:19:33.460
wondered whether on their behalf, if you like,
I could ask for your advice for the people

00:19:33.460 --> 00:19:38.559
who are struggling with those issues as these
STPs, very importantly, move on?

00:19:38.559 --> 00:19:43.590
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Thank you, and I understand
this is a complex question that people are

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:52.770
wrestling with. I would say the first point,
that I think people do appreciate, is the

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:58.120
reason we are doing the STPs is because we
can make a better job of delivering health

00:19:58.120 --> 00:20:05.710
and social care for people by working together
than we can by working in silos. I think,

00:20:05.710 --> 00:20:13.900
be they CCGs or be they acute trusts or mental
health trusts or community services, if they

00:20:13.900 --> 00:20:18.220
say to themselves honestly, 'Can I solve the
challenges that we currently face by just

00:20:18.220 --> 00:20:23.570
working with my own organisation?' the answer
is going to be no. Then the question is, 'Okay,

00:20:23.570 --> 00:20:29.260
so how do I make this work as part of a wider
team? How do we get the service shift from

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:34.390
one location to another which optimises where
patients are receiving their care?' From a

00:20:34.390 --> 00:20:41.860
CCG's point of view, if we want to move away
from a kind of contract-bound struggle between

00:20:41.860 --> 00:20:48.919
where the money goes, and into a joint view
on how we are going to solve it, it requires

00:20:48.919 --> 00:20:56.049
all of the parties to - as I have been going
around the country, I have been using the

00:20:56.049 --> 00:21:01.950
Bob Geldof quote from Live Aid, where he said
to all of the musicians as they turned up

00:21:01.950 --> 00:21:06.400
to the recording session, 'You need to check
your egos in at the door.' There is a bit

00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:12.039
about understanding how organisations work
together, and that balance between, 'My statutory

00:21:12.039 --> 00:21:16.730
accountability that has not gone away,' and
actually, 'Can I fulfil the statutory accountability

00:21:16.730 --> 00:21:21.039
if I try to do it on my own? I think I probably
cannot; therefore, what am I going to share

00:21:21.039 --> 00:21:27.730
and put into partnership to make joint decisions
on our behalf, and building that level of

00:21:27.730 --> 00:21:31.470
trust?' It is going to take some work. It
is not a memo to come out from here saying

00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:37.840
everybody needs to work like this. It does
need to be evolved locally to reflect the

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:43.390
local partnerships. So, they are right to
worry about and ensure that they fulfil their

00:21:43.390 --> 00:21:46.910
statutory obligations, because they have those
statutory obligations, but their best way

00:21:46.910 --> 00:21:54.250
of doing that is to be creative and progressive
about the way in which they share their accountability

00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:59.179
with partner organisations, with a focus on
what does best for patients.

00:21:59.179 --> 00:22:05.380
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: I am sure Matthew did not mean
this, and understands it much better than

00:22:05.380 --> 00:22:13.481
I, but it still sounds incredibly top-down;
that we somehow know the answers and we can

00:22:13.481 --> 00:22:19.149
help these people fix them in their locality,
but actually, this has not been done before

00:22:19.149 --> 00:22:25.570
by all of us, as it were. I was wondering
about the spread, and it is really tricky,

00:22:25.570 --> 00:22:29.610
because it seems to me that local government
in a sense defines itself by being different

00:22:29.610 --> 00:22:34.929
from the next area. We know that the NHS has
always struggled, if it has not been invented

00:22:34.929 --> 00:22:43.770
here, to pick up some new way of doing things.
There has to be at some point a shift in the

00:22:43.770 --> 00:22:49.230
tone where they really do think that they
are in control of their bit, as opposed to,

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:52.750
'Well, we are struggling with this this year,
but next year, they will come along and ask

00:22:52.750 --> 00:22:58.700
us to do something else.' I do not know when
that switch happens, as it were; when they

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:05.330
will believe that you really mean this for
ever, as it were, or for as long as you are

00:23:05.330 --> 00:23:08.070
around, rather than just this year's answer.

00:23:08.070 --> 00:23:20.760
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: In the NHS there is always
a cynicism about this year's initiative, but

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:27.662
I think we are moving beyond that with the
STPs. There is a tension which will not go

00:23:27.662 --> 00:23:33.950
away between there being things that we as
the NHS have an obligation to deliver, which

00:23:33.950 --> 00:23:40.850
you cannot just decide not to do, so this
is a, 'Let us work out how to do this best

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:48.190
locally around a population, and reflecting
the specific needs of your place, your population,

00:23:48.190 --> 00:23:55.399
and there is one NHS budget that cannot be
spent more than that; we have to deliver emergency

00:23:55.399 --> 00:24:01.970
services and elective services, and so on.'
Bringing that tension to bear and turning

00:24:01.970 --> 00:24:09.100
it into a productive relationship is the key
element to this, and I guess there are a couple

00:24:09.100 --> 00:24:14.570
of things. The point I was making about where
the resources are aligned, we have some STPs

00:24:14.570 --> 00:24:24.110
which are led by very senior, very experienced,
very talented teams of leaders locally, but

00:24:24.110 --> 00:24:29.669
in a way, we need to get out of their way,
and so how do we get our resources there aligned

00:24:29.669 --> 00:24:34.269
properly with them in order that they can
direct what needs to be done? Then we have

00:24:34.269 --> 00:24:41.710
other areas that are evolving their ability
to make big change, and what support do we

00:24:41.710 --> 00:24:44.860
need to give them to get them to that place?

00:24:44.860 --> 00:24:53.660
Part of why this is not simple is that we
are trying to say, 'There are 44 different

00:24:53.660 --> 00:25:01.130
STPs, and they are at different levels of
development, they are good at different things,

00:25:01.130 --> 00:25:07.539
and part of our obligation and part of what
we are trying to do is spread the best.' The

00:25:07.539 --> 00:25:11.620
question about spread; the NHS has traditionally
been very poor at spread. You can find almost

00:25:11.620 --> 00:25:16.390
everything that needs to be done being done
somewhere, is kind of the mantra, but not

00:25:16.390 --> 00:25:25.120
next door. So, what Ian and I have been talking
about is how we help the places with the clearest

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:29.899
thought process on things that everybody is
going to end up having to do to accelerate

00:25:29.899 --> 00:25:37.100
and learn and publish, so it becomes easy
to take what was done in one place and tune

00:25:37.100 --> 00:25:40.750
it by 20% to do it in another place, rather
than every time we try and do something, behave

00:25:40.750 --> 00:25:45.740
as though we have invented it for ourselves.
That spread I think is significant, and it

00:25:45.740 --> 00:25:52.320
is the same issue we have on the IT side as
well; of, how do we actually learn and spread

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:56.620
best practice, and therefore improve speed
and lower the cost of delivery and get it

00:25:56.620 --> 00:25:58.340
right first time more often?

00:25:58.340 --> 00:26:05.940
&gt;&gt;John Burn: You sort of just answered my
comment. As you are well aware, not all egos

00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:12.950
are detachable, and in my world of clinical
trials, we have a process called adaptive

00:26:12.950 --> 00:26:16.640
design, where you try a variety of approaches,
and when you check which ones are winning,

00:26:16.640 --> 00:26:20.780
you drop the ones that are not working and
redirect your resources, which I think is

00:26:20.780 --> 00:26:21.780
what you were just describing.

00:26:21.780 --> 00:26:22.780
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes.

00:26:22.780 --> 00:26:27.560
&gt;&gt;John Burn: What that suggests is that we
perhaps need, as a Board and as an organisation,

00:26:27.560 --> 00:26:33.370
to be analysing those STPs and having a clear
segregation into different categories, if

00:26:33.370 --> 00:26:40.000
you like, so that if we see one that is failing,
we can buddy that one perhaps to another organisation

00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:44.260
which might have a slightly different approach
but is a comparable population; rather than

00:26:44.260 --> 00:26:50.230
it being top-down, trying to sort of connect
sideways. Is that how you see that working?

00:26:50.230 --> 00:26:54.789
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I do see it working at
that level, but I also see it working at a

00:26:54.789 --> 00:27:00.159
layer beneath that. Once you dig into the
STPs, there are eight or ten things that they

00:27:00.159 --> 00:27:05.320
are almost all proposing to do. Every STP
is made up of six of the same ten things,

00:27:05.320 --> 00:27:13.750
and what we are trying to do is identify what
those ten things are, make sure that they

00:27:13.750 --> 00:27:18.841
are up and running somewhere quickly, and
that they are properly evaluated and that

00:27:18.841 --> 00:27:24.260
the best versions of them become clear. Ian
can talk a bit about how we are trying to

00:27:24.260 --> 00:27:33.830
create that evaluation process. For instance,
every STP has a piece of it which says, 'We

00:27:33.830 --> 00:27:37.909
have people in hospital who will be better
cared for in a primary community care setting,'

00:27:37.909 --> 00:27:43.450
and therefore has some sort of model of enhancement
and investment in primary and community services

00:27:43.450 --> 00:27:51.399
to create what some call a hub, what the NAPC
calls a primary care medical home. There are

00:27:51.399 --> 00:27:56.120
variations on this. What we want to do is
support some of those to happen quickly, and

00:27:56.120 --> 00:28:00.470
take the lessons out of that, so that everybody
else can go and do it, because 44 inventions

00:28:00.470 --> 00:28:05.799
are not as effective as six inventions copied
by 38 other people. Do you want to add anything

00:28:05.799 --> 00:28:08.350
to that, Ian, about evaluation and learning?

00:28:08.350 --> 00:28:14.390
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Just briefly, we need to scale
up nationally and locally that analytical

00:28:14.390 --> 00:28:20.940
capability, to be able to do the operational
research and to do it in more real-time, so

00:28:20.940 --> 00:28:25.409
that we can do the fast failing and the wider
spread. We had a really good conversation

00:28:25.409 --> 00:28:31.460
at our executive board on that last week,
so it is a really well made point.

00:28:31.460 --> 00:28:37.630
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Matthew, for the
update and the assurance of what is an immensely

00:28:37.630 --> 00:28:47.240
complex but ultimately transformative programme
of strategy planning and operational implementation.

